According to the results, the cPIKAN method performs poorly when using only 1 (i.e., full domain) or 2 subdomains, as indicated by the large relative errors. In these cases, the network fails to provide an accurate approximation of the solution, despite requiring a substantial amount of training time. The performance improves significantly when the domain is divided into 4 subdomains, leading to both lower error and reduced computational time. 
