\IEEEraisesectionheading{\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}}
% inner-engineering of

\IEEEPARstart{T}{he} key aspect of Systems Biology seeks to understand the bigger picture of the complex biological systems that focuses to extract the relevant biological information of an organism at the cellular level. Biological components, such as genes, interact with each other to reconstruct gene regulatory networks (GRNs) from observational gene expression data \cite{bellot2015netbenchmark}. This process unveils a complex web of interactions, shedding light on the underlying patterns that govern gene regulation. Inference of GRNs from gene expression data is crucial for understanding the multifactorial molecular interaction patterns among genes. A gene network consists of interlinked genes, where the expression of each gene influences the activity of other genes in the network \cite{de2010gene}. The natural and effective method for describing GRN involves utilization of graphical and mathematical modeling, often represented using graph-theoretic formalism. Formally, a GRN is represented as a network of nodes and edges, where the nodes represent genes, and edges represent the regulatory interactions between them \cite{guzzi2020biological}. GRN inference involves predicting or computationally guessing the connections among macromolecules by analyzing their relative expression patterns. 

Accurately reconstructed gene networks closely mirror real biological processes that play an important role in post-inference applications. These networks offer valuable insights into gene functions and regulatory mechanisms, enabling pathway analysis to uncover functional relationships. They play a crucial role in facilitating drug-target identification, aiding in the development of targeted therapies. In addition, they improve the understanding of disease progression and effective diagnostics and therapeutic strategies. Precisely, disease gene prioritization is a vital task in isolating genes that play crucial roles in disease mechanisms and can aid in small-molecule design for therapeutic interventions. This success is heavily dependent on the accuracy and completeness of the inferred gene network. 

For decades, researchers have relied on DNA microarray\cite{shyamsundar2005dna} data for GRN inference. However, microarray data are often incomplete and provides a limited view of gene interactions, which makes it inconclusive for large-scale network inference. The advancements in high-throughput and high-resolution Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS) technologies, such as single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq), have provided more precise and comprehensive gene expression data, paving the way for more accurate GRN inference. Although these technologies has its unique strengths, they also come with certain limitations. Common limitations include noisy gene expression data, which often complicates the inference process. Furthermore, the dynamic and non-linear nature of gene-gene regulatory interactions present a significant challenge, as traditional or linear methods often fail to capture the complex relationships comprehensively. In addition, gene regulatory networks tend to be sparse, further reducing the overall accuracy of inference methods. In the case of single-cell technologies, significant dropout events introduce a large number of zero counts in the expression matrix, adding another layer of complexity \cite{talwar2018autoimpute}. These challenges highlight the need for more robust and sophisticated approaches to effectively analyze and interpret gene expression data.


A plethora of supervised and unsupervised GRN inference methods have been developed to uncover intricate relationships within gene networks \cite{huynh2019gene}. Early efforts focused on simpler techniques, such as correlation analysis, mutual information-based methods, differential equations, and more. Significant effort has been devoted to understand the intricate relationships within gene networks \cite{roy2014reconstruction, sebastian2023generic}. However, many methods come with inherent limitations. Therefore, a more reliable GRN inference technique may prove more effective in this case. Numerous intelligent computational techniques have been developed to address this issue.

Over the years, significant progress has been made in developing approaches that incorporate machine learning techniques to infer the network from gene expression \cite{dewey2013gene}. A recent trend in network-science has gained significant momentum in modeling graph-based applications powered by graph neural networks (GNNs). For example, Wang et al., propose GRGNN\cite{wang2020inductive} method to reconstruct GRNs from gene expression data in a supervised and semi-supervised framework. The problem is formulated as a graph classification problem for GRN inference on DREAM5 benchmarks. Q-GAT\cite{zhang2023quadratic} proposes quadratic neurons based on dual attention mechanism for GRN inference. The model is validated by introducing adversarial perturbations to the gene expression data on E. coli and S. cerevisiae datasets. Huang et al.,\cite{huang2023miggri} propose a GNN based model called MIGGRI for GRN inference using spatial expression images that captures gene regulation from multiple images. 

Meanwhile, other methods leverage multi-omics data to improve GRN inference. DeepMAPS\cite{ma2023single} uses an advanced GNN model, i.e., heterogeneous graph transformer by integrating scRNA-seq and scATAC-seq to infer cell-type-specific network. Zarayeneh et al., propose iGRN \cite{zarayeneh2017integration} method that integrates multi-omics information, i.e., CNV (copy number variation) and DNA methylations as well as gene expression data for GRN inference. FSSEM\cite{zhou2020inference} that models GRN inference using structural equation model by integrating gene expression and genetic perturbation data. 

Another line of work also concentrate on Transformer-based architectures for GRN inference. TRENDY\cite{tian2024trendy} applies transformer models to construct a pseudo-covariance matrix in WENDY\cite{wang2024wendy} approach. Then they apply another transformer model that enchances the inferred GRN. STGRNS \cite{xu2023stgrns} is an interpretable transformer-based method for inferring GRNs from scRNA-seq data. It converts each gene pair into a format compatible with transformer models to effectively capture regulatory relationships.


% Unlike GNNs, which are constrained by local message passing, Graph Transformers utilize self-attention mechanisms to capture both local and global interactions among genes.

% All of the aforementioned GRN inference methods rely on Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) and Transformer frameworks, often complemented by multi-omics data integration. Inspired by this, we explore whether incorporating prior knowledge from previously inferred GRNs can further enhance the inference process. GT-GRN achieves this by encoding gene expression data in a high-level feature space and utilizing structured knowledge from existing GRNs to optimize gene representations. Our method employs a graph transformer-based architecture that integrates pre-trained embeddings from multiple source GRNs, gene expression representations, and structured gene interaction data. These elements collectively refine the link prediction process, leading to more accurate and biologically meaningful GRN inference. By jointly leveraging multiple GRNs and gene expression datasets.

To address the challenges in gene regulatory network (GRN) inference, we propose \emph{GT-GRN}, a novel approach that integrates the strengths of both unsupervised inference methods and supervised learning frameworks. Our method combines outcomes from multiple inference techniques to minimize method-specific biases, ultimately deriving a more realistic and biologically meaningful GRN. Unlike existing graph learning approaches that primarily focus on network topology without effectively incorporating gene expression values, \emph{GT-GRN} leverages the latest advancements in Graph Transformer models to enhance GRN inference. \emph{GT-GRN} integrates three distinct representations derived from multimodal input expression networks: (1) topological features, which capture the structural properties of the network; (2) gene expression values, which are crucial for identifying gene interactions; and (3) the positional importance of genes, which reflects their functional relevance within the network. By combining these diverse perspectives, our multimodal framework improves both the interpretability and predictive power of inferred GRNs, making it a robust solution for disease gene prioritization and other biological applications.  

\emph{GT-GRN} is adventageous in multiple sense. 

1) \emph{Multi-Network Data Integration}: Relying on a single data source often fails to capture the full complexity and true dynamics of gene regulatory networks. A more effective approach is to derive a confident GRN by consolidating insights from multiple data sources. Furthermore, while numerous inference models exist, each with its strengths and limitations, leveraging their shared strengths can enhance the prediction of a more reliable and accurate GRN.  

2) \emph{Gene Expression Embedding}: Capturing meaningful representations of gene expression data through advanced embedding techniques can provide a richer understanding of the underlying regulatory mechanisms and improve GRN inference.  

3) \emph{Graph Transformer Frameworks}: Traditional GNNs rely on local message-passing mechanisms to infer graph structures. However, adopting graph transformer-based frameworks offers a more effective encoding strategy by leveraging global attention mechanisms, enabling better capture of complex regulatory relationships in GRNs.  

