GT-GRN provides a powerful framework for advancing GRN modeling and its applications in biological research and disease studies.

\section{Related work}
With decades of effort within the research community dedicated to deciphering gene regulatory relationships from gene expression data, numerous methods have been proposed for reconstructing GRNs\cite{mochida2018statistical}. The methods include regression based methods\cite{haury2012tigress}, mutual information based methods viz. ARACNE\cite{margolin2006aracne}, MRNET\cite{meyer2007information}, CLR\cite{faith2007large}. The supervised methods using machine learning models include Support Vector Machines (SVMs) that  reconstructs biological networks with local models\cite{bleakley2007supervised}, other variants of SVM based methods include CompareSVM \cite{gillani2014comparesvm},
GRADIS \cite{razaghi2020supervised}.

With efficient technological advances, deep learning models have evolved to produce better results than conventional or traditional methods for GRN inference. For instance, Daoudi et al., \cite{daoudi2019deep} propose a deep neural network based model to infer gene regulatory network from the experimental multifactorial data. Turki et al., \cite{turki2016inferring} combines supervised and unsupervised methods to infer GRNs using time-series gene expression data through link prediction. Mao et al., \cite{mao2023gene} applies 3D convolutional neural network (CNN) based on single-cell transcriptomic data by employing a labeling trick during training and inference. Further, a graph based model called GNE\cite{kc2019gne} is a scalable deep learning framework that unifies known gene interactions and gene expression for robust gene regulatory inference. 


% For GNN realted work
DeepRIG\cite{wang2023inferring} advocates global regulatory structure rather than focusing the local regulatory relationships between the pair of genes. The model utilizes a graph auto-encoder model to embed the global regulatory information contained in the graph into latent embeddings to reconstruct gene regulatory network. Another model, GMFGRN\cite{li2024gmfgrn} proposes a deep-learning approach to infer GRNs from scRNA-seq data. At the core, authors employ graph convolutional network (GCNs) for the factorization of single-cell expression matrix to generate embeddings for genes and cells. These embeddings are put into the multi-layered preceptron (MLP) to predict their interactions. Further, Wu et al., propose  MHHGRN\cite{wu2024inference} model to build a multi-view hypergraph by integrating  heterogeneous information on the known TF–target gene interactions and gene expression providing comprehensive representation of gene embedding for GRN inference by incorporating higher order complex regulatory patterns. 




Present research is a step forward towards utilizing multi-source GRN information as well as gene expression data together with graph transformer-based model to learn and infer possible links leading towards GRN inference problem. We therefore formulate the GRN inference problem as a link prediction problem where each network uses a minimal set of edges for learning and prediction. We convert the multi-source GRN inferred networks converting them in text-like sequences to be encoded by masked language model, BERT\cite{devlin2018bert}. Further, a high-level encoded gene expression representations derived from autoencoder\cite{kingma2013auto} are injected into graph transformer to leverage for GRN prediction task.    

\section{Materials and Methodology}
This section describes the datasets utilized to evaluate GT-GRN framework and outlines the formulation of gene regulatory network (GRN) inference in a link prediction setup. The proposed framework for GRN inference is composed of three key modules: 1) Extracting global embeddings via multi-network integration of the inferred GRNs. 2) Encoding gene expression as embedding features. 3)  Fusing gene embedding features with network topological in graph transformer method to predict GRN inference. 4) Gene prioritization module to rank disease-related genes.


\subsection{Global Embeddings via Multi-Network Integration of the inferred GRNs}
The core of \emph{GT-GRN} lies in using multi-network  information to understand gene interactions as prior knowledge. Integrating data from diverse inference methods, each with unique strengths and limitations, provides a holistic and reliable view of the network. This approach overcomes the shortcomings of relying on a single method, enabling robust downstream analysis. Figure \ref{fig:global_emb} illustrates the workflow. 

