Understanding the dynamics of gene expression data involves unraveling the patterns and relationships embedded within large-scale datasets. As profiling technologies rapidly advance, linear models often fail to capture the complex structures present in gene expression profiles. Unsupervised deep learning methods hold significant potential for uncovering meaningful biological signals or patterns from these datasets. In this work, we utilize a variational autoencoder (VAE) \cite{kingma2013auto} to encode gene expression data into compact, meaningful feature representations. VAEs offer a powerful framework for unsupervised learning, enabling the extraction of complex patterns without the need for labeled data. By leveraging the probabilistic nature of VAEs, this approach can uncover latent structures in gene expression data, facilitating downstream analysis and the identification of biologically relevant trends. Figure \ref{fig:feature_generation} illustrates. We provide a brief overview of VAEs and their capabilities.

\subsubsection{Variational Autoencoders(VAEs)}
Variational Autoencoders (VAEs) are a probablistic deep generative class of neural networks designed to reconstruct input data by learning a compressed, low-dimensional representation that effectively characterizes the input. VAEs are unsuperised in nature and generally comprise of two interconnected components: an \emph{encoder} and a \emph{decoder}. 

\begin{itemize}
    \item {\bf Encoder} : maps the input data $x$ to a latent representation space $z$. It approximates the posterior distribution $\alpha(z|x)$ using a neural network. The encoder outputs the parameters, i.e., mean and variance of a multivariate Gaussian distribution $\beta(z|x)$ that serves as an approximation to the true posterior $\alpha(z|x)$. 

    \item {\bf  Decoder} : takes a sample $z$ from the latent space and maps it back to the original data space thus generating a reconstructed $\hat{x}$. This is modeled by the likelihood $\alpha(x|z)$ representing the probability of generating the original data $x$ given the sampled latent variables $z$, often  implemented using a neural network. 
\end{itemize}

During training, the VAE aims to learn the parameters of the encoder and the decoder network parameters by maximizing the Evidence Lower Bound (ELBO) which is given by : 

\begin{equation}
    \text{ELBO} = \mathbb{E}_{\beta(z|x)} [\log \alpha(x|z)] - \text{KL}[\beta(z|x) || \alpha(z)]
\end{equation} 

where, $\mathbb{E}_{\beta(z|x)} [\log \alpha(x|z)]$ is the reconstruction term that reconstructs the input data $x$ given the latent representation $z$.   $\text{KL}[\beta(z|x) || \alpha(z)]$ is the KL (Kullback-Leibler) divergence that quantifies the distance between the approximate posterior $\beta(z|x)$ and the prior distribution $\alpha(z)$.   

To make learning possible as sampling doesn't allow for backpropagation of gradients, the reparameterization trick helps to introduce the deterministic transformation by expressing the random variable $z = \tau(x, \epsilon)$, where $\tau$ is the transformation function for an auxiliary independent random variable $\epsilon$. The above problem can be re-written as: 

 $$  z \sim  \beta(z|x^{(i)}) = \mathcal{N}(z ; \mean^{(i)}, \sigma^{2(i)} I)$$

$$z = \mu +  \sigma \odot  \epsilon \text{; reparamterization trick}$$ 

where, $\epsilon = \mathcal{N}(0, I)$ and $\odot$ is the element-wise product.

\subsection{Graph Transformer for GRN inference}
After deriving the features from the prior available GRNs and gene expression. We utilize the Graph Transformer (GT) to learn these overall features by injecting the underlying structure in the gene interaction graph. As GT is specialized in learning on graph datasets. GT-GRN is illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:GT_GRN}. 

