\section{An Optimal VWAP Execution}\label{sec: VWAP}

In this section, the main benchmark we will be using to track the effectiveness of our strategy is the Volume Weighted Average Price (VWAP).  Let $V_{i,k}$ be the volume of the  $i$-th stock traded at the $k$-th trading period. We, therefore, define the market VWAP for the $i$-th stock after $k$ trades as follows,
\beq{marketVwap}
M_{i,k} \disp =  \frac{\sum_{j=1}^k V_{i,j} S_{i,j}}{\sum_{j=1}^kV_{i,j}},
\eeq
and the trader VWAP of the $i$-th stock after $k$ trades as follows
\beq{traderVwap}
T_{i,k} \disp =  \frac{\sum_{j=1}^k a_{i,j} S_{i,j}}{\sum_{j=1}^ka_{i,j}}.
\eeq
Let $\gamma$ be a discount factor, typically $\gamma$ is such that  $0<\gamma<1$, we define the trader's reward for the $i$-th stock after $k$ trades as follows,
\beq{Reward}
 \nonumber  R_{i,k} =\left \{ \begin{array}{ll} 
 T_{i,k} - M_{i,k},  & $ if $  \quad \sum_{j=1}^k a_{i,j} \leq N_i \\
 0 & $ otherwise$,
\end{array}
\right.
\eeq
and the overall reward for the basket of stocks to trade after $k$ trades as follows,
\beq{TotalReward}
R_{k} & =& \disp\sum_{i=0}^n R_{i,k} =  \sum_{i=0}^n  (T_{i,k} - M_{i,k}).
\eeq
\begin{rem}
Notice that the sequence of rewards $ R = (R_{k})_k$ is bounded because the total number of shares to liquidate is finite, so after a while the trader will complete its liquidation and exit the market.
\end{rem}
\begin{defn}\label{q-function}
 Given a policy $\pi$ with initial action $a_0=a$ and given an initial stock prices  $S_0 = S_{i,0}=s$ and an initial state $\alpha_0=\iota$ of the stationary discrete-time Markov Chain $(\alpha_k)_k$, we define the Q-function of the policy $\pi$ as follows
 \beq{q_function_iterative}
 Q^\pi_p(s,\iota, a)  \disp = \ee\bigg[ \sum_{k=1}^p \sum_{i=1}^n  \gamma^{k-1}(T_{i,k} - M_{i,k})
  \bigg{|} S_0=s, a_0=a, \al_0 = \iota; \pi\bigg],
 \eeq
 \beq{q-function1}
 Q^\pi(s, \iota, a)\disp = \ee\bigg[ \sum_{k=1}^\infty \sum_{i=1}^n  \gamma^{k-1}(T_{i,k} - M_{i,k}) 
   \bigg{|} S_0=s, a_0=a, \al_0=\iota; \pi\bigg].
 \eeq 
 One can obviously see that $\disp \lim_{p\to \infty } Q^\pi_p(s,\iota, a)  = Q^\pi(s,\iota, a) $.\\
The state-action value functions of our control problem are defined as follows
 \beq{valuefunctioniterative}
Q_p(s,\iota, a) \disp= \max_{\pi}Q^\pi_p(s,\iota, a)
\eeq
 and
\beq{valuefunction}
Q(s,\iota, a) \disp= \max_{\pi}Q^\pi(s, \iota, a).
\eeq
We have $\disp \lim_{p\to \infty } Q_p(s,  \iota, a)  = Q(s, \iota, a) $.
\end{defn}

Our main goal is, for each $s\in\cal S$ and  each $\iota\in\cal M$,   to find the optimal policy, say $\pi^*$, such that 
\beq{prob}
Q(s,\iota, a) \disp= \max_{\pi}Q^\pi(s, \iota, a) = Q^{\pi^*}(s, \iota, a).
\eeq




